 - Python `horizon_*.py` implement the OS services (threads, fibres, SVC, file system, HID, time, account, display, audio) and the NVN graphics API. `nvn_*.py` record and replay NVN commands and translate shaders to SPIR-V.
 - C libraries built from `linux/host/`: `libnx2linux_display.so` (SDL3 window and events), `libnx2linux_gpu.so` (Vulkan renderer), `libnx2linux_audio.so` (mixing and output), `libnx2linux_movie.so` (VP8/VP9 by libvpx, Vorbis by stb_vorbis). They are built by CMake in `build-host` under `~/.cache/nx2linux`.
-- `libnx2linux_sync.so` is the start of the kernel synchronisation calls in C (issue 19). It is built by the same CMake project and used only when `NX2LINUX_SVC_NATIVE=1`, which is off by default. In its first step it answers no call, so a run behaves as without it.
+- `libnx2linux_sync.so` answers the four kernel calls on mutex words and condition keys in C (issue 19): `ArbitrateLock`, `ArbitrateUnlock`, `WaitProcessWideKeyAtomic` and `SignalProcessWideKey`. They are one group, all in C or all in Python. It is built by the same CMake project and used only when `NX2LINUX_SVC_NATIVE=1`, which is off by default and is read once at the start of a run. `NX2LINUX_SVC_PROFILE`, `NX2LINUX_SVC_RING` and `--verbose` look inside the Python handling of these calls, so a run that asks for one stays in Python. `WaitSynchronization` and the events are still in Python. The gain in frames per second is unknown until it is measured on Xenoblade 2.
 - Games that render with OpenGL use `gl_vulkan.c`, a GL 4.5 core context that Mesa Zink runs on Vulkan. It is not connected to a game's imports yet (see Balatro below).
 
